    file on disk. Survives reloads and restarts. No backend, no new dependency on the browser side. You still need the
    user to export and send it, and storing usernames on a shared computer becomes your problem.
  - [1mCentral collection[0m — same core, but the adapters batch and POST to a collection endpoint. This is the only option
    that actually answers "debug a production issue" unassisted. It needs a service to receive them (nothing in this
    repo does), plus offline buffering, retry, and a retention policy.
  - [1mBoth, tiered[0m — persist locally always, ship a filtered subset (warn/error) centrally. Best debugging story, most
    moving parts, and it's the one I'd only build if you already have the endpoint.

  Cheap to change later: levels, format, which call sites log, the size of the buffer. Expensive to change later: the
  record schema once something downstream parses it, and the decision to store user data — on-device or central, that's
  a commitment you can't quietly walk back.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m on-device persistence now, with the adapter interface shaped so a shipping adapter drops in later.
  It's buildable in this repo today with no new infrastructure, and it does not pretend you have a log backend you don't
  have. The moment you tell me there's an endpoint to POST to, central collection becomes the better answer and the
  core doesn't change.

  Either way, the redaction rule I flagged goes in the core, not the adapters — [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js:24[39m currently log in
  the scope where the password lives, and persisting that would turn a transient console leak into a stored one.
